  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 \n\nHigh level cirrus clouds will continue to stream over the area this \nthis afternoon, gradually becoming a bit thicker and solid by this \nevening. Overall, conditions are partly sunny with sunshine \nfiltering through the high clouds and seasonably warm\ntemperatures in the 60s. Tonight will feature increasing, \nthickening and lowering cirrus clouds. It will be cool, \nespecially east and north of Binghamton, with lows in the 40s \nfor most locations. \n\nHigh pressure slowly departs to the east tonight with a post \ntropical cyclone pushing in from the southwest. This will bring \nscattered to widespread rain showers to the region tomorrow that \nlast most of the day. There still remains some minor uncertainty on \nthe exact timing of the incoming rain Sunday morning. CAMs suggest \nshowers will move in mid to late morning, and spread over the entire \nregion by mid afternoon. Showers will continue into the evening\nhours before exiting. These rain showers also have potential to\nproduce some thunder, mainly late afternoon into early evening.\nThere may be some lingering rain shows late Sunday night into \nearly Monday morning. Overall rainfall totals have been trending\na bit higher each update, totals across the region range from \nhalf an inch to an inch, with the highest totals over northeast \nPA and the Southern Tier region of NY. East-southeast winds will\nincrease, especially over the ridgetops between 8-15 mph with \ngusts up to 30 mph for the hilltops in NE PA. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nMonday is looking dry, with gradually decreasing clouds as an upper \nlevel ridge pushes in from the west. Winds shift northwesterly and \nmild high temperatures should reach well into the 60s. A surface low \nis forecast to track to our north through the day on Tuesday along \nwith a cold front. Initially this will put our CWA in the warm \nsector, with southwest winds and warm temperatures expected. This \nshould allow surface instability to increase ahead of the frontal \npassage if the timing works out. Model soundings show CAPE values \nranging 200-500 J/Kg, along with 30-40 knots of 0-6km bulk shear. \nPWATs also increase to around 1.4 in, so any convective showers\nthat do form also has the potential to produce heavy downpours.\nIf we do get some breaks of sun, it could be quite warm on Tuesday\nwith highs in the mid-60s to low 70s.\n\nBehind this frontal system the latest guidance keeps it mainly dry \nand seasonably cool for Wednesday and Thursday next week. The next \nchance for rain will be Friday, into Saturday as a potentially deep \ntrough approaches the forecast area. This could bring much cooler \ntemperatures for next weekend, stay tuned.",
